    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Amsterdam sits on the IJ in North Holland, where a 13th-century dam on the Amstel river gave the city its name. Four main canals, the Singel, Herengracht, Keizersgracht and Prinsengracht, curve around the old centre in half rings.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Most of the canal ring was dug in the 17th century, when Amsterdam ships traded from the Baltic to Asia. Merchants built tall, narrow houses with hooks in the gables to hoist goods to the upper floors. The Rijksmuseum holds Rembrandt's Night Watch, and the brown cafés of the Jordaan fill up after work.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "April brings tulips to the flower stalls, and King's Day at the end of the month turns the canals orange. Late spring and September have mild weather and thinner crowds. Anne Frank House tickets are sold online only, so book before you travel.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
